<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
  <title>Atypical Development and Interventions in Middle Childhood</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00049</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>53f6d46d-b1ab-4952-9386-91496a364c24</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to:</para>
    <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Identify the features of developmental disorders and behavioral problems common in middle childhood</item>
    <item>Discuss typical developmental pathways and interventions for common childhood disorders</item>
    </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00002">Adam is seven years old. In his first-grade classroom he is on the edge of his seat or on the floor most of the day. When his teacher asks the students to complete a page of work including two descriptive sentences and a picture, he completes the assignment but usually takes longer than most of his classmates. He has difficulty following the sequence of instructions and his work is often chaotic. He also has difficulty keeping his hands to himself and blurts out the first thing that comes to his mind at any time. He asks to go to the bathroom multiple times each day.</para>
    <para id="para-00003">At lunch and on the playground, Adam is very active and moves around a lot. He engages in many activities at recess even when instructed to stay in only one play area. While eating lunch, he wanders around talking to friends and often doesn’t finish his meal.</para>
    <para id="para-00004">In this next section, you will learn about some of the more common problem behaviors and disorders seen in middle childhood, including attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder, autism spectrum disorder, and internalizing and externalizing problem behaviors and disorders. We also discuss prevention and intervention efforts for these disorders.</para>
    <section id="sect-00002">
    <title>Atypical Development in Middle Childhood</title>
    <para id="para-00005">During middle childhood, developmental disorders are often first recognized as children enter grade school and the environmental demands on their behavior increase.</para>
    <section id="sect-00003">
    <title>Attention Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder</title>
    <para id="para-00006">Because one of the biggest milestones marking the entrance to middle childhood is the start of elementary school, attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is often first diagnosed during these years. Teachers do not and should not diagnose ADHD. But they might often be the first to notice symptoms and refer a student for further assessment (Tahıllıoğlu et al., 2021).</para>
    <para id="para-00007">Children with ADHD often struggle to adjust to school (Sánchez-Perez &amp; González-Salinas, 2017). They may have difficulty academically as well, because they spend more time off-task than classmates who do not have ADHD (Salla et al., 2016). Children with ADHD also are more likely to develop poorer relationships with their teachers (Zendarski et al., 2020). In fact, the recent increase in ADHD diagnoses may reflect, at least in part, the increase in academic pressure children face as they are prepared for standardized testing (Hinshaw &amp; Scheffler, 2014). Increased academic demands require more attention and self-regulation from children. These behaviors are often substantially improved when stimulants like Ritalin and Adderall are prescribed for ADHD (Schwarz, 2017).</para>
    <para id="para-00008">Socioemotional difficulties are fairly common in children with ADHD and can lead to conflicts with peers and with family. Children with more ADHD symptoms are more likely to have a caregiver who is less warm and emotionally available (Breaux et al., 2019). They are also more likely to have ambivalent and disorganized attachment styles and less likely to have a secure attachment to caregivers (Dekkers et al., 2021). The research here is correlational, which means we can’t say that lower caregiver responsiveness caused ADHD. Instead, it may be that parenting and attachment styles are the result of caring for a child with ADHD, which can be challenging. ADHD can also disrupt family life if children are unable to sit through a meal or are disruptive during outings. Educational opportunities for parents to improve their positive parenting techniques show some evidence of improving well-being for children and families with ADHD (Haack et al., 2017).</para>
    <para id="para-00009">Children with ADHD also often have difficulties with peer relationships (Powell et al., 2020). More than half struggle to make and maintain friendships (Gardner &amp; Gerdes, 2015). They may stumble when reading social cues, such as when another child is growing weary of hyperactivity and intensity in their social interactions. As a result, children with ADHD are more likely to be rejected by peers or to experience peer victimization or bullying (Chou et al., 2018). Interventions and preventions that help children with ADHD improve their social and academic skills may reduce other long-term mental health risks, such as depression (Evans et al., 2018).</para>
    <para id="para-00010">ADHD is also comorbid with (frequently occurring with) other disorders including anxiety, depression, and conduct disorder. In fact, approximately 14% of children with ADHD also have depression, and about 25-50% have an anxiety disorder (Pallanti &amp; Salerno, 2020). Children with ADHD are also more likely to have a conduct disorder (27%) or a learning disability (46%) (Larson et al., 2011). When children with ADHD have comorbid mental health issues, they are more likely to have poor academic outcomes. Children with ADHD who experience better environmental support, such as school and community resources, are more likely to show improved outcomes (Shabat et al., 2021). For example, ensuring teachers and parents are effectively trained on the child’s cognitive and sensory needs may improve their overall well-being.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
    <para id="para-00011">Read this article by Kirsten Weir in the APA <emphasis effect="italics">Monitor</emphasis> discussing the <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104EmotionADHD">connection between ADHD and difficulties with emotional regulation</link> to learn more. The author talks about the link between these in terms of both the underlying neurobiology of neurodivergent brains and the environmental demands placed upon individuals with ADHD. Treatments are also discussed.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
    <title>Autism Spectrum Disorder</title>
    <para id="para-00012">Numerous socioemotional challenges often face children with autism spectrum disorder (ASD). One of the core elements of ASD is difficulty with social interactions. Children with this ASD often have difficulties recognizing and understanding the emotions of others (Fridenson-Hayo et al., 2016; Gev et al., 2021). Children with ASD may have limited emotional expressivity in their facial expressions and gestures, and they often use inappropriate vocal intonation when expressing emotions. As a result, peers may have trouble forming relationships with them.</para>
    <para id="para-00013">Caregivers often describe their children with ASD as having difficulty with expressing emotions and poorer emotion regulation skills (Reyes et al., 2020). Although these children show many normal attachment behaviors with their caregivers, they may struggle to use their caregiver as a secure base and source of help in regulating their emotions (Keenan et al., 2017). The challenges of raising a child with ASD can increase parent stress particularly if the child struggles with communication and receptive language skills (Stanojevic et al., 2017). Caregivers of children with ASD have higher levels of stress than parents of children without disabilities (Padden &amp; James, 2017). Among parents of children with ASD, African American report higher levels of stress than European Americans, particularly when they experience less cultural belonging (Williams et al., 2019). Hispanic parents cite the lowest stress, perhaps because they also report higher levels of family cohesion (emotional support among family members) (Valicenti-McDermott et al., 2015). Promoting family cohesion and emotional support for all members of a family is beneficial to reducing familial stress for many types of families and parenting situations.</para>
    <para id="para-00014">Diagnoses of ASD have risen rapidly, and society’s response and understanding have varied (Anthony et al., 2019). On the one hand, there is more inclusion for children with ASD thanks to strong advocacy efforts. On the other hand, individuals with ASD still experience isolation and discrimination. However, research continues, and many programs are working to decrease the stigma and increase acceptance of ASD (Anthony et al., 2019). Intervention programs are also now widely available. Many focus on developing children’s social skills and language in naturalistic settings. Social skills training programs, in particular, have been useful in helping children with ASD develop important capabilities (Odom et al., 2021).</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00005">
    <title>Internalizing Problem Behaviors &amp; Disorders</title>
    <para id="para-00015">Nikstat and Riemann (2020) call mental health disorders focused on the self-- like anxiety, obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD), and depression-- <term id="term-00001">internalizing problem behaviors</term>. There is very little specific data about mental health in middle childhood, often called the missing middle (Voss et al, 2023). However, a marked increase in internalizing problem behaviors and disorders occurs during these years (<link target-id="fig-00001"/>). Entry to school, increased social and emotional demands, emotional dysregulation, angry parenting, maternal mental health, and possible victimization in bullying situations contribute to these conditions, as do adverse childhood events and systemic barriers like lower socioeconomic status (Wang et al., 2018). Internalizing disorders might also be underdiagnosed among marginalized groups, while externalizing and learning disorders might be over-diagnosed (Thyberg &amp; Lombardi, 2022).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00001">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_08_05_Disorder.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>CDC.gov data shows an increase in the diagnosis of internalizing disorders during middle childhood (CDC, 2024).</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00016">In a study with 950 children between 8 and 14, Cervin et al. (2020) found that internalizing disorders like anxiety, OCD, and depression often start in middle childhood. They suggested that preventing and treating anxiety in middle childhood might be a good start to dealing with these disorders in adulthood. Let’s consider some of these disorders.</para>
    <para id="para-00017">According to data from the CDC (2024), between 2016 and 2019 almost 10% of children between 6 and 17 years old were diagnosed with an <emphasis effect="italics">anxiety disorder</emphasis> such as separation anxiety, phobia (an excessive and irrational fear of something like darkness or dogs), social anxiety, or panic attacks (a sudden onset of extreme anxiety accompanied by hyperventilation and a raised heart rate). Often, <term id="term-00002">anxiety</term> is experienced as worry or stress, but some children respond with anger, upset stomach, or inability to sleep.</para>
    <para id="para-00018">Like adults who experience <term id="term-00003">depression</term><emphasis effect="bold">,</emphasis> a mood disorder characterized by persistent feelings of sadness, children who are depressed are not simply sad. Young children often struggle to describe their feelings, but signs that can indicate depression include trouble concentrating at school, physical symptoms like a headache or stomachache, difficulty sleeping, over-sleeping, over-sensitivity, tendency to cry easily, and low self-esteem. Children with depression also often feel little or no joy or pleasure in playing and other activities they previously enjoyed (Schwartz-Mette et al., 2020).</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00006">
    <title>Externalizing Problem Behaviors &amp; Disorders</title>
    <para id="para-00019">Some children have clinical disorders that might be more appropriately considered <term id="term-00004">externalizing problem behaviors</term> or disorders. These are focused more on the social world within which the child engages. Externalizing problem behaviors include things like substance use, truancy, hyperactivity, and impulsivity are considered externalizing behaviors.</para>
    <para id="para-00020">Some emotional disorders are also considered externalizing disorders, <emphasis effect="italics">and oppositional defiant disorder</emphasis> (ODD) is one. The DSM5 defines this disorder as “a pattern of angry/irritable mood, argumentative/defiant behavior, or vindictiveness lasting at least 6 months” (SAMHSA, 2016). Children with it have difficulty obeying authority, sometimes throwing tantrums, and they might also be aggressive. They may be uncooperative and hostile toward their peers, family members, and authority figures.</para>
    <para id="para-00021">Some children might outgrow these behaviors, but in many cases they translate into low academic performance, poor social relationships, and family strife. A diagnosis of ODD can sometimes become a label that follows children as they move into adolescence and early adulthood. There is some concern that Black boys in particular might be over-diagnosed with this disorder because of bias and systemic racism (Ballentine, 2019). Research has identified racial and ethnic disparities in the diagnosis of many mental and behavioral health disorders. In addition, cultural differences in attitudes toward mental health services often mean that Black families are less likely to seek services for mental health and/or behavioral disorders (Ballentine, 2019). Better training for health professionals and those working with children can improve accurate diagnosis and treatment for children across a variety of externalizing and internalizing problem behaviors.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00007">
    <title>Body Image</title>
    <para id="para-00022">The way individuals perceive themselves and feel about their body is <term id="term-00005">body image</term> (Mendo-Lázaro et al., 2017). Body image changes over time, influenced by environmental and cultural experiences.</para>
    <para id="para-00023">Middle childhood is an important time for the development of body image due in part to the many changes taking place socially, emotionally, and in the brain. As children enter middle childhood, they begin to internalize messages regarding body image such as the “thin ideal” seen in girls, but not boys, as young as age 6 (Rice et al., 2016). In middle childhood children begin making social evaluations about others and become sensitive to the evaluations others are making of them (Preckel et al., 2013). As a result, negative body images may form during these years, including concerns about weight and spurring behaviors directed at improving physical appearance (Neves et al., 2017). Among 9- to 12-year-old children in Spain, 75% of both boys and girls were not satisfied with their body and 20% were very dissatisfied (Mendo-Lázaro et al., 2017).</para>
    <para id="para-00024">Having a negative body image can also lead to mental health problems and eating disorders (Vaquero et al., 2013). Helping children develop realistic attitudes about body image can protect them against the development of these mental health disorders (Mendo- Lázaro et al., 2017).</para>
    <para id="para-00025">A healthy lifestyle is linked to a better body image in children. Children with a higher level of physical fitness generally have a more positive body image than those who are less physically fit, although for children with a negative self-concept, engaging in physical exercise can be a potential risk factor for eating disorders (Mendo- Lázaro et al. 2017).</para>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00008">
    <title>Resources and Interventions in Middle Childhood</title>
    <para id="para-00026">Many children benefit from multiple sources of support. First, mental health assessment and treatment need to be more widely available. CDC data shows that only about 60% of children with mental illness are diagnosed or receive treatment (CDC, 2024). Treatment is called for when children are in distress or when their academic and social functioning is not optimal. But the conditions that lead to or worsen mental problems must be addressed and preventive programs established to have the greatest impact for all children. Other children may not need mental health services but still benefit from supports to help them optimize their learning and development. What are some treatments, supports, and preventive paths to supporting children’s varied needs? Here we discuss those supports provided in a school setting, and mental health treatments and resources available for children.</para>
    <note class="life-hacks" id="note-00002">
    <title>Do You Want to Make the Difference in the Life of a Child?</title>
    <para id="para-00027">Many children can benefit from having a positive role model or just another caring adult in their life. Children with internalizing or externalizing problem behaviors and those who struggle with academics might especially benefit from having a mentor (DuBois et al., 2018; McQuillin et al, 2015). Mentoring relationships can be either informal, occurring naturally as children interact with adults in their lives, or formal like the organized mentorship programs of Big Brothers Big Sisters of America. Child mentoring programs are designed to provide children with a volunteer nonparental positive role model, matching them with someone who can regularly spend time with them.</para>
    <para id="para-00028">One of the major benefits of mentoring programs is that they are widely accepted and as a result serve many children who would otherwise not receive any services (Hagler et al., 2019). For example, caregivers from marginalized groups are more likely to allow their children to take part in a mentoring program than to opt for services from professionals like mental health practitioners (Vázquez &amp; Villodas, 2019). The success of mentoring programs depends on volunteers who are willing to donate a few hours a month to develop a relationship with a child.</para>
    <para id="para-00029">Are you interested in making a difference in the life of a child? Consider starting by looking up the local non-profit organizations that serve youth in your city or region.</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00009">
    <title>School-Based Supports</title>
    <para id="para-00030">Children with disabilities or other issues that affect their learning are entitled to free and appropriate education by law. In every U.S. state, therefore, parents can request that the school test their child for learning difficulties, or a teacher can escalate a request for a child to be assessed on socioemotional issues with parental consent ([citation placeholder]). School psychologists conduct the assessment to decide what services would be most beneficial for the student (NASP, 2024). These services are then outlined in the <emphasis effect="bold">individualized education plan</emphasis> or IEP, a legal document written at a meeting of school administrators, teachers, parents, and specialists like school counselors, social workers, speech therapists, and school psychologists. The IEP team will monitor the student’s progress and adjust the services as needed. IEPS are also regularly revisited and revised as necessary.</para>
    <para id="para-00031">The IEP sets out how the child will be assisted – either by an aide in the classroom, with services provided after school, with sessions with a therapist, or with other appropriate interventions and accommodations. The specific services a child receives vary quite a bit based on individual needs. A child with a mild speech impairment, for example, might see a speech or language therapist once a week, while another child might spend the day in a special education classroom or have an aide assigned to them the entire time they are in school. Public school districts are required to have in-house professionals like counselors, school social workers, school psychologists, and resource specialists, or they may contract with regional centers or agencies for consultants to work with children and families.</para>
    <para id="para-00032">Another way schools can provide learning supports for children is through a 504 plan, designed to assist students with disabilities in general education classrooms (Edsource, 2022). These plans are also individualized for each student and are meant to ensure they have equitable access to learning. Some students might have both an IEP and a 504 plan, but they are separate documents.</para>
    <para id="para-00033">Approximately 14% of U.S. children receive some special education services in public schools (National Center for Education Statistics, 2022). Because these are provided free of charge for any eligible student, many children benefit from therapies they would not otherwise receive because they are too expensive for many families.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00010">
    <title>Mental Health Support</title>
    <para id="para-00034">Social media, mass violence, political polarization, and COVID-19 have all contributed to an increased the need for mental health services in children. For example, a study of children in China found high rates of anxiety and depression as a result of the pandemic (Duan et al., 2020). This study also found that factors such as smartphone or internet addiction, residence in urban areas, and reliance on emotional-focused coping styles, such as avoidance or denial, were associated with these higher rates of depression and anxiety. A study with Swedish children found that problematic school experiences contributed to increased mental health issues like internalizing and externalizing problem behaviors, ADHD, and social problems among 9- to 12-year-old children (Waenerlund et al., 2016). Children can receive mental health support in a variety of ways, both in and out of school.</para>
    <para id="para-00035">For example, many schools now offer curricula or programs designed to support social-emotional learning among children (Wallender et al., 2020). They may have counselors, social workers, and/or school psychologists on staff to work with children. The COVID-19 pandemic brought mental health issues into sharp focus. For example, the Los Angeles Unified School District (the second largest public school district in the nation) hired more than 800 school social workers in 2022/23 in response to this need. Other schools have also recognized the necessity of providing additional mental health supports to children.</para>
    <para id="para-00036">A resource gaining more support in schools is school social work. Social workers trained as mental health professionals work with children dealing with problems like anxiety or personal loss, at the request of teachers, parents, or even children themselves. School social workers are also a resource for teachers, helping coordinate the school’s socioemotional learning curriculum, conducting mini-lessons on topics like bullying or relaxation programs, and supporting school attendance and participation. Another professional who can provide mental health support to children in a school setting is the school counselor. Although many school counselors primarily help students succeed in school and begin career planning, they can also provide support for students through short-term counseling and by advocating for students as needed.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00003">
    <para id="para-00037">Watch this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TEDHarris">TED talk where Nadine Burke Harris talks about how childhood trauma affects lifelong health</link> to learn more. This talk illustrates the multiple contexts and issues for children’s health and development in general.</para>
    </note>
    <para id="para-00038">Mental health supports available outside school include private counselors. Another possibility is support groups for children with specific needs such as depression, groups that help children develop social skills, and groups that help them cope with specific traumas such as the death of a parent or divorce. Healthy lifestyle factors can also be helpful. For example, the CDC recommends healthy eating, sleeping, exercise, and mindfulness and/or relaxation therapy for healthy functioning in children. Every child’s mental health needs are unique, but caregivers have many options for supporting them.</para>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section class="summary" id="sect-00011">
    <list id="list-00002">
    <item>Socioemotional difficulties are common in children with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD). They include insecure attachments and challenges with peer relationships.</item>
    <item>Children with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) also have socioemotional challenges, including difficulties with social interactions, emotion regulation, and emotional expression.</item>
    <item>Internalizing problem behaviors &amp; disorders like anxiety, obsessive compulsive disorder, and depression are all disorders having to do with the self and can be diagnosed in children.</item>
    <item>Externalizing problem behaviors &amp; disorders like oppositional defiant disorder, impulsivity, and hyperactivity are often diagnosed in middle childhood and involve how the child interacts with the outside world.</item>
    <item>Individualized educational plans or IEPs describe the school-based services provided to support students with learning difficulties.</item>
    <item>Other interventions for children include counseling services in school (from school counselors or social workers), private counseling, and support groups for specific needs.</item>
    </list>
    </section>
